{
  "id": "sit_to_stand_5x",
  "name": "Five-times sit-to-stand",
  "unit": "s",
  "higherIsBetter": false,
  "summary": "How many seconds it takes to stand up from a chair five times as fast as you safely can, without using your arms. It reflects leg strength and how easily you get up from a seat. A shorter time is better.",
  "howTo": [
    "Use a firm chair without arms, seat about 43–47 cm high, with its back against a wall so it can't slide. Write down the chair height and use the same chair next time.",
    "Sit near the front of the seat, feet flat on the floor about shoulder-width apart, arms folded across your chest.",
    "Try one single stand first. If you can't stand up without using your arms, stop here: don't do the timed test.",
    "Someone says 'Ready… Stand' and starts the stopwatch on 'Stand'.",
    "Stand up fully (hips and knees straight) and sit back down, five times, as fast as you safely can. Arms stay folded the whole time.",
    "Stop the clock when you are fully standing after the fifth rise. (This is how the SHARE study timed it. Some studies stop when you sit down after the fifth rise, which adds time, so always time it the same way.)",
    "Do it once. Record the time to one decimal place and whether you used your arms or stopped early."
  ],
  "safety": [
    "Do it with someone nearby, with the chair against a wall and nothing around you to trip over.",
    "Skip it, or ask a physiotherapist first, if you have recently had hip or knee surgery, have sharp joint pain, get dizzy when you stand up, or have been told not to do exertion like this.",
    "Stop at once if you feel dizzy, short of breath, chest pain or joint pain.",
    "Wear flat, non-slip shoes or go barefoot on a non-slip floor."
  ],
