  "limits": [
    "Times depend on chair height, arm position, whether the clock stops when you stand up or sit down after the fifth rise, practice, and tiredness from earlier tests. Comparisons only make sense with the same setup each time.",
    "The norms come from different countries and protocols and don't agree well: at 50–54 the median is about 9–10 s in Europe (SHARE), 8.1–8.8 s in the Asian pool, and 7.1 s (men) to 11.0 s (women) in the small Colombian sample's 50–59 group. Treat a percentile as a rough guide.",
    "It measures getting up from a chair, which mixes leg strength, power, balance, joint pain and confidence. It can't tell you which one is limiting you.",
    "A slow time is linked to higher risk in groups of older people. It doesn't diagnose anything, and it doesn't predict what will happen to you.",
    "No study found here shows that improving your own time changes your health risk.",
    "Norms for people under 50 rest on smaller or less consistent data (Asian pooled cohorts, a 393-person Colombian study, a 172-person Dutch study)."
